\section*{A uniform gap in the periodic spin-one antiferromagnetic chain}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. The pure spin-one antiferromagnetic Heisenberg chain is claimed to have a size-independent positive gap on even periodic rings.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} For spin-one matrices satisfying \([S^a,S^b]=i\varepsilon_{abc}S^c\), \(\sum_a(S^a)^2=2I\), use the even periodic ring
\[
 H_L=\sum_{j=0}^{L-1}\boldsymbol S_j\cdot\boldsymbol S_{j+1},\qquad\boldsymbol S_L=\boldsymbol S_0.
\]
Let \(\gamma_L\) be the difference between its first two distinct energies. The source claims a unique ground state for even \(L\ge60\), and
\[
 \gamma_L>\frac4{105}\log\frac{80}{79}\quad(L\ge60),\qquad
 \gamma_L>\frac{\log20}{784}\quad(L\ge2304),
\]
for even lengths. Therefore \(\liminf_{L\to\infty,\ L\text{ even}}\gamma_L\ge\log20/784>0\). Simultaneously similar realizations with the same spin normalization have the same spectral conclusion.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Auxiliary twists express partition functions as spatial moments. Two purity comparisons yield a gap criterion, and two finite initializations start an iterative improvement that makes the criterion uniform in ring size.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The pure spin-one antiferromagnetic Heisenberg chain is claimed to have a size-independent positive gap on even periodic rings. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

